{"name":"Banksia","hex":"#a6b29a","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Banksia","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A taxonomic genus within the family Proteaceae – banksias, with characteristic flower spikes and fruiting cones and heads, native to Australia."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A suburb of Sydney in the Bayside council area, New South Wales, Australia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Banksia"}],"entries":[{"title":"Banksia","blocks":[{"text":"Banksia is a genus of around 170 species of flowering plants in the family Proteaceae. These Australian wildflowers and popular garden plants are easily recognised by their characteristic flower spikes, and woody fruiting \"cones\" and heads. Banksias range in size from prostrate woody shrubs to trees up to 30 metres (100 ft) tall. They are found in a wide variety of landscapes: sclerophyll forest, (occasionally) rainforest, shrubland, and some more arid landscapes, though not in Australia's deserts."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Banksia_in_the_Blue_Mountains.jpg/330px-Banksia_in_the_Blue_Mountains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banksia","wikidata":"Q131258"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Banksia_spinulosa_1.jpg/500px-Banksia_spinulosa_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Banksia_spinulosa_1.jpg/500px-Banksia_spinulosa_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Young Banksia inflorescence showing flower buds developing in pairs","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABanksia_spinulosa_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Banksia_marginata_immature_and_mature.jpg/500px-Banksia_marginata_immature_and_